<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wu Ding</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century-bce">13th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wu Ding was a king of the Shang dynasty who reigned for several decades in the thirteenth century BCE from the capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yinxu">Yinxu</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anyang">Anyang</a>. The earliest known oracle-bone inscriptions date from his reign and record wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tufang">Tufang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guifang">Guifang</a> and Qiang, royal hunts and rituals. His consort <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fu-hao">Fu Hao</a>, whose intact tomb was found in 1976, led armies on his behalf.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wu-ding.jpg" alt="Wu Ding" width="320" height="495" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wang Qiji of the Ming Dynasty, Wanli engraved &quot;Three Talents Picture Club · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%AD%A6%E4%B8%81.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
